<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- pr-doctor (#2303): drive every open PR toward `merged`, automatically.
         The doctor itself is agent-infra/scripts/pr-doctor.py, farmed to
         $HOME/.pi/agent/scripts/ by pi-bootstrap/setup.sh's fleet-scripts farm.

         ⛔ WHY THIS TEMPLATE EXISTS AT ALL. The code had been shipped and farmed
         for days while NO job definition existed for it: on 2026-10-21
         `bash scripts/install-launchd.sh` with its status verb listed every other job as
         `installed=loaded` and this one as `NOT INSTALLED`, `orchestrator-bringup.sh
         status` printed `pr-doctor … no job definition … MISSING`, and the farmed
         script never ran. A missing job is invisible in exactly the way that costs
         most: nothing fails, so nothing is filed. The label is the one the live box
         was hand-armed under (`com.tortoise.pr-doctor`), so installing this template
         REPLACES that hand-made agent with the tracked one — same label, same
         script, now reproducible from source. That replacement is the point.

         THE TARGET IS THE FARMED WRAPPER. setup.sh's fleet-scripts farm copies
         pr-doctor-machine.sh (fleet_srcs) and lib/repo-set.sh (lib_srcs) to that path
         BEFORE the launchd convergence step, which is what makes the `<string>` resolve
         on a fresh box.
         ⛔ IT IS NOT AN ALWAYS-INSTALLABLE JOB, AND THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY IT WAS
         (review round 22, P1). It is a `{{TORTOISE_REPO}}`-GATED template — like
         branch-reaper, worktree-reaper and worktree-hygiene, and UNLIKE pi-reap-orphans,
         pi-cache-reap and lane-liveness, whose names all resolve from the INSTALLER'S OWN
         environment and really are always installed. ⛔ THE REAL RULE IS ROLE VS ENVIRONMENT,
         NOT "names no placeholder" (round 24, P2: this line said "no placeholder that can
         resolve EMPTY", which is FALSE — `install-launchd.sh` has a dedicated PATH skip
         arm, measured to SKIP pi-cache-reap and lane-liveness when every PATH entry is
         unreadable). A name resolved from the box's ROLE (`TORTOISE_REPO`, `SWARM_ROOT`,
         `AI_*`) gates the template; a name resolved from the installer's own environment
         (`HOME`, `PATH`) does not — so "always installed" holds for those three only while the
         installer's PATH renders non-empty. The gate fires BEFORE `verify_targets`
         (`install-launchd.sh`
         returns SKIP from the placeholder loop), so on a box that resolves no tortoise
         checkout this job is SKIPPED at rc 0 and the target check never runs at all.
         ⛔ AND THAT IS A REAL LIMIT OF THIS CHANGE, RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT IMPLIED:
         the wrapper is the ONLY consumer of the repo set, so on a box serving other repos
         (agent-infra plus a second repo — the shape §1 of #2281 is about) creating
         `repos.conf` changes nothing until a doctor unit exists for THAT box. The unit that
         serves it is owed, and it must not be gated on a tortoise checkout. Until it lands
         the set is correct and unread on those boxes — an installed-but-inert state this
         file would rather name than hide.
         WHERE THE PLACEHOLDER DOES RESOLVE, install-launchd.sh hard-fails a job whose
         target does not resolve, and that check is deliberately NOT defeated here: a bare
         `install-launchd.sh` after a farm that did not deliver FAILs this job instead of
         installing a dead one, which is the correct verdict and the same one every farmed
         job gets.
         The interpreter is resolved BY THE WRAPPER (`python3` on PATH, then
         /usr/bin/python3, then /usr/local/bin/python3), because this unit's
         ProgramArguments names `/bin/bash` + the farmed wrapper and launchd gives a job
         no PATH of its own. The installer's PYTHON_BIN placeholder is not used here —
         it belongs to the units whose argv[0] IS the interpreter.
         ⛔ AND THAT NAME IS DELIBERATELY UN-BRACED (review round 23, P1). `install-launchd.sh`'s
         placeholder gate greps the RAW template — COMMENTS INCLUDED — so writing a braced
         placeholder token anywhere in this file, even while explaining that the placeholder
         is NOT used, ADDS IT AS A SKIP GATE: measured, the braced form made the installer print
         `SKIP com.tortoise.pr-doctor: unresolved placeholder(s) PYTHON-BIN — is this a swarm
         host?` (the emitted line names PYTHON_BIN in braces there; it is spelled here with a
         hyphen ON PURPOSE, see below) on a box whose only missing name was the one this unit
         does not need. ⛔ THE
         INVARIANT IS NOT "keep prose names unbraced" (round 24, P2 — the TORTOISE_REPO mention
         above names one braced, legitimately, because this template already requires that name as
         a value): a braced name in prose is SAFE only when the template already
         requires that name as a VALUE token, because then it adds no new gate. A name the
         template does NOT already require must stay unbraced in prose. When in doubt,
         `grep -o '{{[A-Z_]*}}'` this file and diff it against `origin/main`.
         (An earlier version of this paragraph justified that by claiming the placeholder
         points at a non-existent venv on a non-swarm box; `install-launchd.sh` actually
         falls back to `command -v python3` when the venv is absent, so that install would
         NOT hard-fail, and the claim was wrong. The reason above is the real one.)

         WHY 30 MINUTES. One pass is a real read of every open non-draft PR plus up
         to MAX_FIX/MAX_REVIEW load-gated worker spawns, with each gh call bounded at
         180s — it is a DISPATCH pass, not a poll. 1800s matches the interval the
         live box was hand-armed at, and it is the cadence the pass's own cooldown
         and per-PR idempotency were built for: a tighter timer would mostly re-read
         state it just wrote, and a looser one would let a red PR wait an hour for its
         fixer. There is no RunAtLoad on purpose — the same shape as the reapers
         above: `launchctl kickstart` (or the next sync) starts it immediately when an
         operator wants a pass now.

         WHERE THE OUTPUT GOES. pr-doctor writes its own bounded pass log at
         $HOME/.pi/agent/state/pr-doctor.log and its PASS VERDICT at
         .../pr-doctor.status.json; in the multi-repo (`all-repos`) mode the wrapper
         runs, each repo ALSO gets `pr-doctor.<owner>-<name>.log` and
         `pr-doctor.<owner>-<name>.status.json` beside them, so one repo's verdict cannot
         stand in for another's. The launchd capture below is the third surface and
         the one its own code names — a pass that dies before it can write either
         still leaves its crash text here (a launchd job's stderr is the only place
         an interpreter-level death lands), which is what makes an import error
         distinguishable from an honest RED verdict. The wrapper's own mode line
         (`set: … grading N repo(s)`, or `DEGRADED — NO-REPO-SET …`) is on the same
         capture, which is what makes a box with no repo set visible instead of quiet.

         GH: PATH carries the fleet's gh-wrapper dir first (rendered by
         install-launchd.sh with the unreadable pi shim dir removed, #1719/#427), and
         HOME stays the real home so the doctor's state dir is the fleet's, not
         launchd's.

         ⛔ THE REPO IS NOT NAMED HERE AT ALL ANY MORE (agent-infra#2281 §1). This unit used to
         assert its repo in argv — the literal `repo` flag followed by the tortoise slug —
         which was correct when the box ran one repo and became the defect the moment it ran two:
         on a cell whose config declares `OR=daniel-ospina/agent-infra` while its controller was
         started for tortoise, the doctor logged the repo as sourced from ARGV (its emitted line reads
         "repo: &lt;slug&gt; (from &lt;source&gt;; checkout &lt;dir&gt;)", where the source for that path is
         the argv repo flag) on
         EVERY pass and the second repo's queue was read by nothing. #2280 fixed WHICH repo the
         resolver picks; it could not give a multi-repo box more than one, because #2280 deliberately
         shipped `PR_DOCTOR_REPOS` with NO default set — there was no authoritative machine list to
         read (§4). There is one now: `scripts/lib/repo-set.sh` reads the machine's repo SET, and
         `scripts/pr-doctor-machine.sh` — what this unit execs — grades EVERY repo in it.

         ⛔ STILL NOT `GH_REPO`. `GH_REPO` is a box-GLOBAL selector (a pushed `keys.env` once
         exported `GH_REPO=daniel-ospina/tortoise` and `gh` obeyed it for every command on the box,
         #2235), so it is not evidence and the doctor pins it — in that direction only — for the
         children it spawns. ⛔ AND NOT THE `repo` FLAG EITHER, for the same class of reason one
         level up: a flag in one machine-wide plist is a SECOND place that says which repos this box
         serves, and the whole point of §4 is that there is one. `REPO` below stays as the box's own
         checkout EVIDENCE, and it is used in TWO places, not one: on the degraded path (no set yet),
         and — with a set present — as the fallback `_checkout_that_proves()` adopts for an entry whose
         OWN checkout proves nothing. In both it is adopted only when its git `origin` PROVES the slug
         being graded, so the plist cannot make the doctor read another repo's worktrees. What the pass
         then does with an unprovable checkout is the doctor's to say (it may adopt this one and spawn,
         or grade read-only); on macOS, where launchd cannot read `~/Documents`, the degraded path
         usually ends in the wrapper's refusal with the file to create — see the next block.

         ⛔ WHAT HAPPENS ON A BOX WITH NO SET (the migration state), IN TWO CASES, and this is the
         distinction that matters because THIS template's default checkout lives under `~/Documents`:
           * the wrapper CAN still name a repo (`PR_DOCTOR_REPO`, `OR`, or a `REPO` whose git origin
             proves one) → it runs the single-repo form and LOGS `DEGRADED — NO-REPO-SET` with the
             exact file to create;
           * it CANNOT → it REFUSES (exit 2), naming both fixes.
         On macOS the launchd job usually lands in the SECOND case without a set, because it has no
         Full Disk Access under `~/Documents` and so cannot read the checkout's origin. So the
         accurate statement is not "it keeps working"; it is "it says, every pass, in the launchd
         capture, exactly what to create". Create the set and the same unit grades every repo in it
         with no reinstall — which is the whole point, and the reason the refusal names the file.

         ⛔ A SET THAT EXISTS BUT DOES NOT VALIDATE IS A REFUSAL (exit 2, named). Dropping a line the
         reader cannot parse would grade a shorter set, and a shorter set reads exactly like a
         smaller machine. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.tortoise.pr-doctor</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <!-- The FARMED wrapper (fleet_srcs), which reads the machine repo set (lib_srcs) and grades
             every repo in it. No repo is named in this argv — see the block above. -->
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/pr-doctor-machine.sh</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}</string>
        <!-- The CHECKOUT (not a slug), and it is used in BOTH modes — see the block above: as the
             degraded path's evidence AND as the `_checkout_that_proves()` fallback for a set entry whose
             own checkout proves nothing. In both, adopted only if its `origin` PROVES the slug. Do not
             drop it as "degraded-only": that would turn such an entry from spawn-in-this-checkout into
             read-only. -->
        <key>REPO</key>
        <string>{{TORTOISE_REPO}}</string>
    </dict>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>1800</integer>
    <!-- ⛔ /tmp, like every other template in this directory: launchd opens these files BEFORE exec,
         and nothing in install-launchd.sh creates $HOME/.pi/agent/state. A capture path whose parent
         may not exist fails at bootstrap, and the job that loses its only crash surface is this one. -->
    <!-- Captured to /tmp, not $HOME/.pi/agent/state: launchd opens these files BEFORE exec
         and nothing creates the state dir for it, so the hand-armed unit's
         $STATE/pr-doctor.launchd.log is DEAD from the moment this installs.
         NOTE (review round 22, P2): `heartbeat-loop.sh` still prints that old $STATE path on
         its launchd arms. The /tmp choice here is a recorded decision with the reason above,
         so the inconsistency is named rather than reversed: read /tmp/pr-doctor.launchd.log
         for passes installed by THIS unit. -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/pr-doctor.launchd.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/pr-doctor.launchd.err</string>
</dict>
</plist>
